<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->

This section is designed to describe more user-defined options to those who are already familar to the basic 
usage of ExaGRyPE. Users are recommend to read and understand the python/C++ scripts of the code before they
continue here. 

# Adding New Physics Scenarios
For no-matter spacetime, different physics scenarios are represent by different initial
conditions. The initial conditions of ExaGRyPE can be assigned via direct function, e.g., what we do for guage
wave scenario in \c InitialValues.cpp

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
void applications::exahype2::ccz4::newScenario(
  double * __restrict__ Q, // Q[64+0],
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&  x,
  double t
) {
  Q[0]  = 1.0;
  Q[1]  = ...... 
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For more complicated initial conditions (e.g. black holes), users can introduce their own calculating library, 
and include them in before generate and build the Peano project. We also recommend to define a corresponding 
C++ flag to avoid unnessary include files when other scenario is picked.  

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
peano4_project.output.makefile.add_cpp_file( "MyICCalculators/Calculator1.cpp" )
peano4_project.output.makefile.add_cpp_file( "MyICCalculators/Calculator2.cpp" )
......
peano4_project.output.makefile.add_CXX_flag( "-DIncludeMyCalculator" )
......
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
.......
#ifdef IncludeMyCalculator
#include "MyICCalculators/UseMyCalculator.h"
#endif
.......
void applications::exahype2::ccz4::newComplicatedScenario(
  double * __restrict__ Q, // Q[64+0],
  const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&  x,
  double t
) {
  MyCalculator(Q, x, t);
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Postprocessing on-the-fly

Users can define postprocessing function if they wish to manipulate evolving variables during the 
timestepping, or to extract more information from the system through the material variables (The material
variables are additional variables the user can ask the code to output with the evolving system into 
snapshots to inspection).

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
MySolver._auxiliary_variables = 3 #set the number of material variables you need

MySolver._my_user_includes += """
  #include "../MyPostprocessingfunctions.h"
"""

MySolver._preprocess_reconstructed_patch = """
	MyPostprocessingfunction(...);
	.......
"""

MySolver.create_data_structures()
MySolver.create_action_sets()
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

\c CCZ4.py and \c CCZ4Helper.py contain multiple postprocessing functions that users
can take as examples to implement their own ones.

# Particles as Probes and Tracers

@todo There used to be a picture probes.png which somehow disappeared. Should be reintroduced.

<!-- @image html probes.png -->

ExaGRyPE can have particles inserted in the domain for multiple features. Those particles
do not have physics interaction and act as data probes or object tracers. To add particles 
to the domain, you can create a particle set using

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracer_particles = project.add_tracer( name="Tracer",attribute_count=59 )
init_action_set =  exahype2.tracer.InsertParticlesByCoordinates( 
    particle_set=tracer_particles, coordinates=[[0,0,8.0],[0,0,-8.0],[8.0,8.0,0],[-8.0,-8.0,0]])
init_action_set.descend_invocation_order = 0
project.add_action_set_to_initialisation( init_action_set )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     

besides specifying their coordinates directly, you can also import them from a file:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  
#init_action_set = exahype2.tracer.InsertParticlesFromFile( 
    particle_set=tracer_particles, filename="t-design.dat", scale_factor=abs(offset[0])*0.8)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  

The next step is to associate the particles with your solver using the kernel you specify, for static
data probes, the kernel only need to specify how you project neighbor field solution to your particle
location, the most common one would be \c projectAllValuesOntoParticle_piecewiseLinear

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
tracer_action_set=exahype2.tracer.FiniteVolumesTracing(tracer_particles,
                                                       my_solver,
                                                       project_on_tracer_properties_kernel="::exahype2::fv::projectAllValuesOntoParticle_piecewiseLinear",
                                                       )
tracer_action_set.descend_invocation_order = my_solver._action_set_update_cell.descend_invocation_order+1
project.add_action_set_to_timestepping(tracer_action_set)
project.add_action_set_to_initialisation(tracer_action_set)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

By switching to a \c projectAllValuesOntoParticle_piecewiseLinear_explicit_Euler kernel, you can ask 
particle to follow certain vector field in the solution (or any variables as long as the total number equal
to dimension number), this method can be used to trace the object flowing in the field, i.e., track the black
holes moving in the mering binaries

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
tracer_action_set=exahype2.tracer.FiniteVolumesTracing(tracer_particles,
                                                       my_solver,
                                                       project_on_tracer_properties_kernel="::exahype2::fv::projectAllValuesOntoParticle_piecewiseLinear_explicit_Euler",
                                                       projection_kernel_arguments="""marker,
                                                       {{PATCH_SIZE}},
                                                       {{NUMBER_OF_UNKNOWNS}}+{{NUMBER_OF_AUXILIARY_VARIABLES}},
                                                       fineGridCell{{SOLVER_NAME}}Q.value,
                                                       fineGridCell{{SOLVER_NAME}}CellLabel.getTimeStepSize(),
                                                       {17,18,19},
                                                       {-1,-1,-1},
                                                       *p
                                                       """)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

The output of particles would contain two parts: the first part is the usual vtu files shows the locations of particles
and variables recorded by them, the second part is the csv file that you can control with 

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
dump_action_set=exahype2.tracer.DumpTracerIntoDatabase(
    particle_set=tracer_particles,
    solver=my_solver,
    filename=args.path+"Tracer-BHTracker",
    number_of_entries_between_two_db_flushes=1000,
    output_precision=8,
    data_delta_between_two_snapsots=1e16,
    position_delta_between_two_snapsots=1e16,
    time_delta_between_two_snapsots=0.02,
    clear_database_after_flush      = True
    )
dump_action_set.descend_invocation_order = my_solver._action_set_compute_final_linear_combination.descend_invocation_order+2
project.add_action_set_to_timestepping(dump_action_set)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

Each particle ouputs its label, coordinates, time and recorded data as one-line entry each time, and if the dumping criteria
is satisfied, available criteria including count of entries, data changes, position changes and time changes. If the recorded
entires exceed the threshold your specified (1000 in the example above), the code will output a csv file for current data 
and clear the data base. 

We recommended to output csv file every now and them during the simulation, it will help to reduce memory footprint, and it is
also safer, as a crash does not trigger automatically dumping, i.e., all particle data still in the memory will lost in this case.

